  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nThis is a very complex forecast over the next 24 to 36 hours as\na deepening trough moves out of the Intermountain West into the\nPlains. Water vapor imagery early this morning shows this \nsystem centered roughly over Utah and somewhat disorganized \nstill. A subtropical jet is surging across the Southwest into \neastern New Mexico and the northwest Texas Panhandle, with \nconvection already noted in that region. The polar jet is \nrounding the base of the trough currently and remains discrete \nfrom the subtropical jet initially. Broad and deep ascent will \nincrease later this morning and this afternoon, associated with \nthe subtropical jet moving into the area. This forcing may get \nan initial round of showers and storms to develop in the \nnoon-3pm time frame. CAPE is going to be limited for this entire\nevent and that will be the running theme throughout the next \n24-36 hours. But if sufficient surface based CAPE can develop \nduring the day, and it isn't constantly eroded from the strong \nforcing overspreading the area, these initial cells could become\nsevere. This seems unlikely though. What seems more likely is \nstorm development along the front through central-eastern Kansas\nduring the afternoon, where instability is maximized just ahead\nof the boundary. These storms will likely quickly become linear\nor semi-discrete clusters as the whole area moves east from the\nlate afternoon into the evening hours. As this is occurring, \nthe low- level jet strengthens, enhancing the curvature of the \nlow-level hodograph and lengthening it overall across eastern \nKansas and into far western Missouri, increasing the potential \nfor embedded tornados within the line. Ensemble mean MUCAPE\npeaks around 500 J/kg in far eastern Kansas and western \nMissouri. Again, very limited but may be sufficient given the\nshear. With this line of storms moving into our western zones \nas early as 02-04Z and continuing east through 06-08Z, we may be\ndealing with a damaging wind and QLCS tornado risk well into \nthe overnight hours before the system loses steam in the pre- \ndawn hours. \n\nTuesday continues to look unsettled and may pose a sneaky severe \nrisk with some instability ahead of the slow moving front late \nin the morning to early afternoon. The NAM still shows a local \nmax of instability through the forecast area just ahead of what \nmay be a triple point in west-central Missouri. Shear is \nextremely strong though and so strong it could rip apart \nupdrafts that do try to develop. So it's something to keep an \neye on but seems lower probability at this point with the \ngreater risk for any additional severe weather well southeast of\nthe forecast area. \n\nThe forecast for the later half of the week and through the weekend \nwill feature cooler, closer to normal temperatures. Early next week, \nin the Sunday to Monday time frame, deterministic models show a \nstrong shortwave moving through the middle of the country. The GFS, \nin particular, moves very cold air aloft over the region with this \nshortwave. With 925mb temperatures near 0C, this could support some \nwintry precipitation. Only about 15-20% of ensemble clusters show \nanything close to a robust closed system early next week.",
